.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-09-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_set_header \- set a response header, replacing every field of that name
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_resp_set_header(chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                                        const char *name,
                                        const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets a response header to a single value.  When fields with the same
name already exist, the first one takes the new value in place and
keeps its position, and the others are removed, so the response then
carries exactly one field of that name.  The name comparison is
case-insensitive, as RFC 7230 requires.  To send an additional field
of a name instead, such as a second Set-Cookie, use
chttpsvr_resp_add_header(3).  The fields go on the wire in the order
of the calls that first set each of them.

Letter case of the name: the stored header name keeps the letter case
of the FIRST call, and the letter case of every later call is
ignored.  HTTP/1.1 header names are case-insensitive on the wire, so
this does not affect correctness, but a caller that reads the stored
header names back directly needs to account for this behaviour.

None of the three arguments may be NULL; a NULL in any of them gives
ccol_invalid_args and changes nothing in the response.  name must
also be a non-empty token that contains only tchar bytes, as RFC 7230
SS3.2.6 defines them: every byte must be an ASCII letter, an ASCII
digit, or one of "!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~".  A zero-length name has no valid
form on the wire.  A name containing any other byte, for example a
space or a literal ':', is not by itself a way to inject a CRLF, but
it does make a structurally malformed wire line that a strict parser
downstream can misread.

value must not contain a CR byte or an LF byte.  The server writes
both name and value onto the wire exactly as they are, as
"name:value\er\en", with no further escaping, so a CR or LF inside
value would let whoever controls the data that the caller reflects
(for example a query parameter, a path parameter or an echoed request
header placed into a response header) inject extra header lines or
split the response in two.  That attack is HTTP response splitting,
or CRLF injection.  The function rejects a CR or LF with
ccol_invalid_args instead of silently stripping it or truncating the
value.

The function accepts a "Connection" header and checks it like any
other name, but the server never sends it: the server always emits
its own Connection header, which shows whether the server really
keeps the connection open afterwards.

A "Content-Length" value must be one or more ASCII digits whose number
fits in a signed 64-bit integer; any other value gives
ccol_invalid_args.  Whether the server sends it depends on the
response.  A response that sends a body carries the length of the body
that chttpsvr_resp_write and its sibling functions really wrote, and
never the value that you set, because that length is the framing on
the wire.  A 304, and a response to HEAD for which the handler wrote
no body, send no body whatever the field says; they carry the
Content-Length that you set, which is the length that a 200 to a GET
would carry (RFC 9110 section 8.6), and none at all when you set none.
A 1xx and a 204 never carry one.  This is the rule of Go's net/http
server.

A "Date" header that you set is sent exactly as given and replaces
the Date that the server otherwise adds, so the response carries
exactly one.  If you set none, every final response carries the
current time in UTC, in the IMF-fixdate form of RFC 9110 section
5.6.7, such as "Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT".  A 1xx response, and
the interim "100 Continue", carry none.  This is the rule of Go's
net/http server.  A second call with the same name, in any letter
case, whether through this function or through
chttpsvr_resp_add_header(3), replaces the value, so no sequence of
calls can produce two Date lines.

The function rejects a name of "Transfer-Encoding", in any letter
case, outright, rather than accepting it and filtering it out later.
This server never transfer-codes a response body, because it frames
every response with Content-Length, so it cannot obey a
Transfer-Encoding header that a caller sets.  Such a header on the
wire would also sit next to the Content-Length header that this
function computes, over a body that nothing transfer-coded.  That
framing is ambiguous (RFC 7230 SS3.3.3), and an intermediary that
gives Transfer-Encoding priority over Content-Length can misparse it,
which is the same class of hazard that the handling of Connection and
Content-Length above prevents.  chttp_request_set_header rejects
Transfer-Encoding in the same way on the client side.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when resp, name or value is NULL; when name is empty
or holds a byte outside the RFC 7230 tchar set; when value holds a CR
byte or an LF byte; when name is "Transfer-Encoding" in any letter
case; or when name is "Content-Length" and value is not a number as
described above.
ccol_not_enough_memory when the underlying allocation fails.
A failed call changes nothing in the response.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_add_header (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_set_status (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write (3)
